14.4 02 — Databases and tables
CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS my_db;
USE my_db;            -- every later statement refers to my_db
SHOW TABLES;
CREATE TABLE Users (UserID varchar(30), BadgeNum int, FirstName varchar(255),
                    LastName varchar(255), Age int, OtherAttr float);
DESCRIBE Users;       -- the schema: columns, types, keys, defaults
The notebook then repeats the same with the connector (Users_py) to show the commit rule in practice: CREATE TABLE through the cursor needs db.commit(), while USE/SHOW don’t.

20.6 From bag to DataFrame
Bags are typically the E and T of an ETL: read messy input, clean and flatten it, then hand a table to something more efficient. to_dataframe() needs flat records with the same keys:
def flatten_account(r):
    return {'id': r['id'], 'name': r['name'],
            'first_transaction_id':     r['transactions'][0]['transaction-id'],
            'first_transaction_amount': r['transactions'][0]['amount']}

ddf = records.map(flatten_account).to_dataframe()
ddf            # prints structure and npartitions only: no data is computed
ddf.head(10)   # computes just enough to show 10 rows
Other exits from a bag: to_textfiles, to_avro, to_delayed.
